<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ostrava</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ostrava is the third-largest city of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a>, on the Oder near the Polish border, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a> meets <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-silesia">Czech Silesia</a>. From the nineteenth century it grew into the centre of a major coalfield and of heavy industry around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vitkovice-ironworks">Vítkovice ironworks</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> mining in the city ended in 1994 it became the capital of the Moravian-Silesian Region.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ostrava.jpg" alt="Ostrava" width="320" height="245" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: A. Novotná · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ostravakolaz.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
